-- title:
--   Points of a cube deviate from its centre by less than half the side
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $h \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Write $A_h(u)$ for the cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ (Definition 2.5). If $x \in A_h(u)$, then for every coordinate index $i$,
--
--   $$|x_i - u_i| < \frac{h}{2}.$$
--
--   Thus the cube is the *open* cube in the maximum norm: membership forces a strict coordinatewise bound. This is the basic unpacking lemma for cubes, and it is the step by which cube membership is converted into the metric estimates $A_h(u) \subseteq B(u, \tfrac{h}{2}\sqrt d)$ and the like.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No positivity is assumed of $h$. For $h \le 0$ the cube is empty (when $d \ge 1$), so the statement is vacuously true there; the hypothesis $x \in A_h(u)$ carries all the content.
-- source:
